    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The lands of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> ruled by the popes as temporal sovereigns from the Frankish donations of the 750s until 1870.</li><li>Papal control was often weak, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> popes made the state a real power and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> an artistic capital.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a> and then Italian unification dismantled it; Vatican City, created in 1929, is its last remnant.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a> in 1506, personally led his army in winter 1511 and entered the captured town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mirandola">Mirandola</a> through a breach in its walls.</p></aside>
    </section>

