    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-gregory-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_the_Great_with_the_Holy_Spirit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">British Library, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The temporal power of the popes grew out of the collapse of Byzantine authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. From the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> (590–604) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> administered the extensive estates of the Roman church, fed the city and negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and the Byzantine Duchy of Rome increasingly looked to the pope rather than to distant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The quarrel over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-iconoclasm">Iconoclasm</a> in the eighth century widened the gap. In 728 the Lombard king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#liutprand-king-of-the-lombards">Liutprand</a> handed the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sutri">Sutri</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-gregory-ii">Pope Gregory II</a>, an act often seen as the first territorial grant to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a>. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aistulf">Aistulf</a> seized Ravenna in 751 and threatened Rome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alps">Alps</a> and in 754 met the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, whom he anointed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a>. Pepin defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lombards">Lombards</a> in 754 and 756 and gave the pope the lands of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a>, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#donation-of-pepin">Donation of Pepin</a>. In 774 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the Lombard kingdom and confirmed the grant to Pope Hadrian I, and in 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned him emperor in Rome. Around this time the forged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a>, claiming that the first Christian emperor had granted the pope authority over the West, was produced; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> proved it a forgery in 1440.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#bologna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bologna.jpg" alt="Bologna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torri_di_Bologna,_Bologna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fabio Ciminelli, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Papal States were largely agrarian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-campagna">Roman Campagna</a> was given over to great estates and grazing, increasingly malarial and thinly populated, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#umbria">Umbria</a> grew grain, wine and olives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a>, the richest city of the state, had a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> industry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>’s oldest university. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> itself was exceptional. Its population fell to perhaps 20,000–30,000 in the fourteenth century but recovered to about 100,000 by 1600, and the city lived largely on the presence of the papal court, pilgrims and the revenues of the universal church. Jubilee years, first proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-boniface-viii">Boniface VIII</a> in 1300, brought great crowds of pilgrims. The discovery of alum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tolfa">Tolfa</a> in the 1460s gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> a valuable monopoly, and from the sixteenth century the popes financed themselves through the sale of offices and a funded public debt organised in <em>monti</em>. Society was dominated by baronial families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#colonna-family">Colonna</a> and Orsini and by the relatives of successive popes, who acquired land and titles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> of Rome, present since antiquity, were confined to a ghetto by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-paul-iv">Paul IV</a> in 1555 and remained subject to restrictions until 1870. Catholic religious life shaped the calendar, charity and education, while in the nineteenth century the state was widely regarded as one of the most backward in Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ostia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ostia.jpg" alt="Battle of Ostia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ostia">Battle of Ostia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raphael_Ostia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Raphael, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The popes were weak military rulers and usually relied on others to protect them: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, the Normans of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, who became papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#melfi">Melfi</a> in 1059, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a>. After Arab raiders sacked the basilica of Saint Peter in 846, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-leo-iv">Leo IV</a> enclosed the Vatican with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#leonine-wall">Leonine Wall</a>, and a papal-allied fleet defeated another Arab force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ostia">Battle of Ostia</a> in 849. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenary</a> companies and condottieri. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Cardinal Gil Álvarez Carrillo de Albornoz</a> reconquered the papal provinces in 1353–1367 with mercenary armies and a ring of fortresses. Around 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, subdued the lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#romagna">Romagna</a> with French help, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> personally led his troops, recovering Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a> in 1506 and besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mirandola">Mirandola</a> in 1511. Julius founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pontifical-swiss-guard">Pontifical Swiss Guard</a> in 1506. Papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galley">galleys</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marcantonio-colonna">Marcantonio Colonna</a> served at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wars-of-castro">War of Castro</a> of 1641–1644 showed the limits of papal arms against small Italian princes. In the nineteenth century the small papal army, reinforced by foreign volunteers including the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zouaves">Zouaves</a>, was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#piedmont">Piedmont</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-castelfidardo">Castelfidardo</a> in 1860 and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> only with French help, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mentana">Mentana</a> in 1867.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#egidian-constitutions"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egidian-constitutions.jpg" alt="Egidian Constitutions" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#egidian-constitutions">Egidian Constitutions</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegidianae_Constitutiones,_1571_-_002.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fondazione Mansutti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Papal States were an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elective-monarchy">elective monarchy</a>. From 1059 popes were elected by the cardinals, a two-thirds majority was required from 1179, and from 1274 elections took place in a closed conclave. The pope ruled as both spiritual head of the Catholic Church and temporal sovereign, and the two roles were never separated. For much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> papal control outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> was weak: cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a>, Perugia and Viterbo governed themselves as communes, local lords ruled as papal vicars, and in Rome itself a commune with its own senate challenged papal rule from 1143. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iii">Innocent III</a> (1198–1216) recovered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spoleto">Spoleto</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#march-of-ancona">March of Ancona</a> and organised the state into provinces under rectors. During the residence of the popes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#avignon">Avignon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Cardinal Albornoz</a> restored order and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egidian-constitutions">Egidian Constitutions</a> in 1357, which remained the basic law of the state until 1816. Finances were managed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#apostolic-camera">Apostolic Camera</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-sixtus-v">Sixtus V</a> reorganised the central government in 1588 into congregations of cardinals, including one for the administration of the state, and provinces were governed by cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#legatus">legates</a> and delegates. Nepotism, by which popes enriched relatives and made them cardinals, was a recurring feature until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-xii">Innocent XII</a> restricted it in 1692. Clergy dominated the administration until the end, which made reform and lay participation difficult.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sistine-chapel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel.jpg" alt="Sistine Chapel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sistina-interno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Snowdog at Italian Wikipedia., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Papal States gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> a territorial base that allowed it to act as an independent power in European politics, but they also entangled it in Italian wars and dynastic ambitions. The Donation of Pepin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coronation-of-charlemagne">coronation of Charlemagne</a> in 800 created a lasting partnership between the papacy and the western empire and shaped the political theory of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baroque">Baroque</a> popes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> became the artistic capital of Europe: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-sixtus-iv">Sixtus IV</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-julius-ii">Julius II</a> commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raphael">Raphael</a>’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in 1506, was completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carlo-maderno">Carlo Maderno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a> in the seventeenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-gregory-xiii">Gregory XIII</a> introduced the Gregorian calendar in 1582, now used worldwide. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> blamed the papacy’s temporal state for keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> divided, and in the nineteenth century the existence of the Papal States became the main obstacle to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, the so-called Roman Question. Their loss in 1870 freed the papacy from the burdens of territorial government and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929, left it with the small sovereign state of Vatican City, which allows the Holy See to act in international affairs as a sovereign entity today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-castelfidardo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo.jpg" alt="Battle of Castelfidardo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-castelfidardo">Battle of Castelfidardo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Gallucci_La_Battaglia_di_Castelfidardo_palazzo_comunale_di_Castelfidardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">it:Giovanni Gallucci (b. 1815 - d.: unknown), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Papal States were dismantled in stages between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-unification">Italian unification</a>. Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#avignon">Avignon</a> and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-pius-vi">Pius VI</a> to cede the Legations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferrara">Ferrara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#romagna">Romagna</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-tolentino">Treaty of Tolentino</a> in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-pius-vii">Pius VII</a> returned in 1800, but in 1809 Napoleon annexed the remaining states to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> and the pope was held captive until 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored almost all the territories in 1815, but papal government was unpopular among the educated classes, and revolts in 1831 were put down by Austrian troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-pius-ix">Pius IX</a> began as a reforming pope in 1846, but after the murder of his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pellegrino-rossi">Pellegrino Rossi</a> in 1848 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaeta">Gaeta</a>, and a Roman Republic was declared in 1849 until French troops restored him. In 1859–1860 the Romagna rebelled, the papal army was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-castelfidardo">Castelfidardo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#umbria">Umbria</a> voted to join the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> survived under French protection until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> forced France to withdraw its garrison. On 20 September 1870 Italian troops entered Rome through the breach at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porta-pia">Porta Pia</a>, and Rome became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> in 1871.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Papal States made the pope a temporal ruler of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> for over eleven centuries, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> the independence and wealth that sustained its role in European politics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a> art, while keeping Italy divided until the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> in 1870 and leaving Vatican City as its last remnant.</em></p>

